      <!-- Section 7: The Final Verdict -->
      <h2>7. The Final Verdict: Which City Wins Your Itinerary?</h2>
      <p>
        If your schedule strictly limits you to choosing only one imperial city, match your travel persona to the verdict below:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Pick Marrakech if:</strong> You are visiting Morocco for the first time, you love rooftop cocktails and boutique dining, you want easy day trips to the High Atlas or Atlantic coast, you value accessible navigation, or you are traveling on a shorter 4-to-5 day getaway.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pick Fes if:</strong> You are an architectural purist, photographer, or history enthusiast who craves an authentic, unvarnished look into the Arab-Berber medieval world, you want to experience the world's greatest living car-free medina, and you prefer your budget to stretch 30% further.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <!-- FAQ Section Accordion for Search Engine Rich Results -->
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions: Marrakech vs Fes</h2>
      <div class="roam-faq-accordion" style="margin: 28px 0;">
        
        <details class="roam-faq-item" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.1); padding: 16px 0;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; cursor: pointer; color: #111;">Is Fes or Marrakech better for first-time visitors?</summary>
          <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Marrakech is widely considered the smoother entry point for first-time visitors. The streets are wider, high-end tourism infrastructure is expansive, English is widely spoken in riads, and international flight routes into Marrakech Menara Airport (RAK) are plentiful. Fes is more intense and deeply medieval, making it rewarding for travelers who are comfortable navigating complex historical environments.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="roam-faq-item" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.1); padding: 16px 0;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; cursor: pointer; color: #111;">Is Fes cheaper than Marrakech?</summary>
          <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Yes. Fes is approximately 30% to 40% more affordable than Marrakech across riad accommodations, local dining, and artisanal handicrafts. A palatial historic riad in Fes typically costs between $80 and $200 per night, whereas a luxury boutique equivalent in Marrakech commands $180 to $450+.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="roam-faq-item" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.1); padding: 16px 0;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; cursor: pointer; color: #111;">How many days do you need in Marrakech vs Fes?</summary>
          <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            We recommend 3 to 4 days for Marrakech to cover the medina souks, major palaces, gardens, and an excursion into the High Atlas Mountains. For Fes, 2 to 3 days provides ample time to immerse yourself in Fes el-Bali, visit the Chouara Tannery, and take a half-day trip to the Roman ruins of Volubilis.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="roam-faq-item" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.1); padding: 16px 0;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; cursor: pointer; color: #111;">How do you travel from Marrakech to Fes?</summary>
          <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            The direct ONCF train connects them in about 6.5 hours for $25–$35 USD. However, travelers who want to see Morocco's landscapes hire a private driver to make a 3-day or 4-day desert road trip crossing the High Atlas Mountains, Dades Valley, and Erg Chebbi dunes before arriving in Fes.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="roam-faq-item" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.1); padding: 16px 0;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; cursor: pointer; color: #111;">Which medina is safer: Fes or Marrakech?</summary>
          <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Both medinas are safe from violent crime due to strict Moroccan tourist police patrols. In Fes, the main challenge is getting disoriented in the 9,000 alleys and dealing with persistent faux guides near the tanneries. In Marrakech, the challenge is navigating reckless motorbikes in narrow lanes and persistent souk bargaining. In both cities, booking an official licensed guide for your first morning eliminates 95% of tout friction.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="roam-faq-item" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.1); padding: 16px 0;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.1rem; cursor: pointer; color: #111;">Can you visit both Marrakech and Fes in one week?</summary>
          <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: #444; line-height: 1.6;">
            Absolutely. By booking an open-jaw flight (arrive in Marrakech RAK, depart from Fes FEZ or Casablanca CMN), you can spend 2 nights in Marrakech, 2 nights driving across the Atlas and Sahara, and 2 nights in Fes without backtracking.
          </p>
        </details>

      </div>

